      "id": "1770402787373598000_0_579",
      "text": "He went on to win the Stanley Cup with the Stars the next year, scoring a controversial goal on the Buffalo Sabres' Dominik Hasek to clinch the Cup for Dallas. The Blues ended the 1990s as the only NHL team to make the playoffs for the entire decade, although a Stanley Cup title still eluded the franchise.\nIn addition to the aforementioned numbers, the NHL also retired Wayne Gretzky's number 99 from use for all of its members teams, including the Blues, at the 2000 NHL All-Star Game. Gretzky had previously played for the Blues in 1996, although the Blues did not retire his number prior to its league-wide retirement.",
      "distance": 329.1134033203125,
      "metadata": {
        "from_clapnq": false,
        "doc_id": "1770402787373598000_0"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "1770402787373274000_0_511",
      "text": "During the next season, he was traded back to the Winnipeg Jets for their fifth-round choice (who ended up being Alexei Vasiliev) in the 1995 NHL Entry Draft. After signing as a free agent with the Los Angeles Kings in 1996, he did not finish the season with them before being traded to the Pittsburgh Penguins for Glen Murray. He finished his career in Chicago.\n== Awards and achievements ==\nStanley Cup champion \u2013 1994 (New York Rangers)\nUnited States Hockey Hall of Fame 2012\nNational Polish-American Sports Hall of Fame 2004",
      "distance": 329.6990966796875,
      "metadata": {
        "from_clapnq": false,
        "doc_id": "1770402787373274000_0"
      }
    }
  ]
